description:
Why is there no speedup for the for loop?
code and output is as following:

library(quickr)

fn <- function(n,total) {
  declare(type(n=double(1)),type(total=double(1)))
  for (i in 1:n) {
    total=total+i
  }
  total
}

system.time({  
  total=fn(1e8,0)
})
#  用户  系统  流逝 
# 19.57  0.04 19.89 

system.time({
  fn_native <- function(n,total) {
    for (i in 1:n) {
      total=total+i
    }
    total
  }
  total_native = fn_native(1e8,0)
})

#  用户  系统  流逝 
# 19.79  0.03 19.92

`quickr` has few dependencies, but sometimes package developers may want to avoid having to import them and/or `quickr` itself.

When the Fortran is already generated, `quickr::quick` just builds a small wrapper call to `.External`.  Thus it could be possible to vendor a `quick()` or similarly-named function which generates this wrapper by default, but calls `quickr::quick()` behind a `requireNamespace()` guard when updating code with devtools.

e.g. we are using the following in a package that we recently sped up with `quickr` (this goes in `aaa.R` so it is loaded first):
```r
quick <- function(fun, name) {
  if (nzchar(Sys.getenv("DEVTOOLS_LOAD"))) {
    if (requireNamespace("quickr", quietly = TRUE)) {
      return(quickr::quick(fun, name))
    }
    warning("quickr is not installed; returning uncompiled function")
    return(fun)
  }

  body(fun) <- as.call(c(
    quote(.External),
    as.name(paste0(name, "_")),
    lapply(names(formals(fun)), as.name)
  ))
  fun
}
```

So a feature request might be to provide a helper/setup/init function to vendor this code.  Otherwise, perhaps this issue can serve as documentation of one possible approach to keeping `quickr` in `Suggests` rather than `Imports`.

It would be good to be able to handle expressions like this better:

```r
right <- if (ilo < np) pos[ilo + 1L] else pos[np]
```

Right now compiled `if` expression have no return value, so we have to write:

```r
if (ilo < np) {
  right <- pos[ilo + 1L]
} else {
  right <- pos[np]
}
```

We should be able to dispatch from the C entry point to specialized subroutines with almost no impact in speed, and not much added complexity.

The feature can be opt-in, and with syntax like this:

```r
multiply <- function(x, y) {
  declare({
    type(x = double(1 | n))
    type(y = double(1 | n))
  })
  x * y
}
```

which would create 4 specialized subroutines, and a jump table in the C bridge.

The above example would be how to generate specialized subroutines for different variable axis sizes, but we could also at the same time support variable rank and type by moving the `|` out a level.

```r
multiply <- function(x, y) {
  declare({
    type(x = double(1 | n) | integer(1 | n))
    type(y = double(1 | n) | integer(1 | n))
  })
  x * y
}
```
or more simply for the same effect:

```r
multiply <- function(x, y) {
  declare({
    type(x = numeric(1 | n))
    type(y = numeric(1 | n))
  })
  x * y
}
```

This would generate 16 specialized subroutines, and a jumptable with 16 entries in the c bridge.
```r
expand.grid(
  x = c("dbl", "int"),
  y = c("dbl", "int"),
  x = c(1, NA),
  y = c(1, NA)
)
```
